            solution: `<p>The array data is a 3D array of type int [3][4][5]. Each element is a 2D array of size<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>[</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math></p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>block_ptr</mi></math> points to a 2D block. In the call <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>pw_analyze</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>block_ptr</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></math> the pointer moves to the second block, i.e., <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>data</mi><mo>[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Inside the function, it points to this block.</p>
<p>*base gives the 2D array [4][5].<br />&nbsp;(*base)[idx] accesses the row at index 1.<br />&nbsp;&amp;(*base)[idx] gives the address of that row, so <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>row_ptr</mi></math> points to that row.</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>row_ptr</mi></math> + 1 moves to the next row.</p>
<p>(*base + idx) also points to the same row as row_ptr. After typecasting to int (*)[5], both pointers are of the same type.</p>
<p>The subtraction (<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>row_ptr</mi></math> + 1) - (int (*)[5])((*base) + idx) gives the difference in number of rows between the two pointers, which is 1.</p>
<p>Final Output: 1</p>`
