            answer: 10,
            solution: `<p>On a little-endian system, the least-significant byte of a multi-byte value is stored at the lowest address.</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>arr</mi><mo>[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>01020304</mn><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;Its 4 bytes in memory (low &rarr; high) are: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>04</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>03</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>02</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>01</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Casting arr to char * lets us read individual bytes.</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo>[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>04</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo>[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>03</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo>[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>02</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo>[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mn>01</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>Sum</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math></p>`
