            type: "NAT",
            text: `<p>A complete binary tree with n = 15 nodes is stored in an array (1-indexed). The nodes at even indices contain even values, and the nodes at odd indices contain odd values.</p>
<p>How many parent-child pairs (parent index, child index) exist where the parent is at an even index, and the child is at an odd index?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                ``,
                ``,
                ``,
                ``,
            ],
            answer: 3,
            solution: `<p>In a 1-indexed binary tree array:</p>
<ul>
<li>Children of node at index i are at 2i and 2i+1</li>
<li>Parent of node at index i is at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&#8970;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8971;</mo></math></li>
</ul>
<p>We need pairs where the parent index is <strong>even,</strong> and the child index is <strong>odd</strong>.</p>
<p>For a parent at even index p, its children are at 2p (even) and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math> (odd).</p>
<p>So every even-indexed parent has exactly <strong>one odd-indexed child</strong> (the right child at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math>), as long as that child exists within n=15.</p>
<p>Even indices from 1 to 15 that are internal nodes (have children): an internal node exists at index i if <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8804;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>so</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8804;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Even indices in range <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p>
<p>For each:</p>
<ul>
<li>Parent at 2: children at 4 (even), 5 (odd) <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&#8594;</mo></math> 1 odd child</li>
<li>Parent at 4: children at 8 (even), 9 (odd) <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&#8594;</mo></math> 1 odd child</li>
<li>Parent at 6: children at 12 (even), 13 (odd) <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&#8594;</mo></math> 1 odd child</li>
</ul>
<p>Each gives exactly 1 valid pair. Total = <strong>3</strong></p>`
