            text: `<p>Consider a min-heap containing exactly <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math> nodes for some k (a perfect binary tree). All elements are distinct.</p>
<p>The 3rd minimum element of the heap can be located at which of the following sets of possible indices in the 1-indexed array?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>only</mi></math></p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>only</mi></math></p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p>`,
            ],
            answer: "B",
            solution: `<p>In a min-heap:</p>
<ul>
<li>The minimum element is always at the root (index 1).</li>
<li>Since all elements are distinct, the 2nd minimum must be one of the root&rsquo;s children:</li>
</ul>
<p>2nd minimum<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&isin;</mo><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p>
<p>Now consider the 3rd minimum. It can be:</p>
<ol>
<li>The other child of the root, OR</li>
<li>A child of the node containing the 2nd minimum.</li>
</ol>
<p>Case 1: 2nd minimum at index 2</p>
<p>Then possible candidates for the 3rd minimum are:</p>
<ul>
<li>sibling at index 3</li>
<li>children of index 2 <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math> indices 4 and 5</li>
</ul>
<p>Possible positions:<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p>
<p>Case 2: 2nd minimum at index 3</p>
<p>Then possible candidates for the 3rd minimum are:</p>
<ul>
<li>sibling at index 2</li>
<li>children of index 3 <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math> indices 6 and 7</li>
</ul>
<p>Possible positions: {2,6,7}</p>
<p>Combining both cases:<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p>
<p>Hence, the 3rd minimum element can appear at any of these indices.</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mo>.</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced><mtext>​</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math></p>`
