            text: `<p>Consider the following statements about sorting algorithms:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>S1:</strong> Merge sort is always stable and requires O(n) extra space for arrays</li>
<li><strong>S2:</strong> Heap sort is in-place and stable</li>
<li><strong>S3:</strong> Quick Sort with the median-of-three pivot strategy has worst-case O(n log n) time</li>
<li><strong>S4:</strong> Counting sort runs in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">O</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>)</mo></math> time, where k is the range of input, and is stable if implemented carefully</li>
<li><strong>S5:</strong> Radix sort on d-digit numbers using a stable sort for each digit runs in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></math> time and is stable</li>
</ul>
<p>Which combination of statements is <strong>TRUE</strong>?</p>`,
            image: "",
