            marks: 2,
            neg: 0,
            type: "MSQ",
            text: `<p>A connected weighted undirected graph G has <strong>n vertices, m edges</strong>, and all edge weights <strong>distinct</strong>. Let T be the unique MST of G.</p>
<p>Which of the following statements is/are <strong>TRUE</strong>?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>The maximum weight edge in any cycle of G does not belong to T</p>`,
                `<p>The minimum weight edge of G always belongs to T</p>`,
                `<p>If we increase the weight of every edge by the same constant c<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&gt;</mo></math>0, the MST may change</p>`,
                `<p>The second minimum spanning tree (MST with second smallest total weight) can be obtained by swapping exactly one edge of T with a non-tree edge</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "B", "D"],
            solution: `<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>A:</strong> By the cycle property of MSTs in any cycle, the maximum weight edge is never in the MST (since removing it doesn't disconnect the cycle, and it's the most expensive). <strong>TRUE</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>B:</strong> By the cut property, the minimum weight edge crosses some cut and must be in every MST. More directly: if the global minimum edge were not in T, adding it to T creates a cycle where it's the minimum contradiction since the cycle's maximum would be removed, not the minimum. <strong>TRUE</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>C:</strong> Adding constant c to all edges. Consider two spanning trees with weights <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mtext>W</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><mtext>W</mtext><mo>&#8322;</mo><mo>.</mo></math> After adding c to each of the n-1 edges, the new weights are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mtext>&#160;</mtext><mi>and</mi><mtext>&#160;</mtext><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>.</mo><mtext>&#160;</mtext></math>The difference W₁-W₂ is preserved. <strong>The MST does not change</strong> since relative ordering is preserved. <strong>FALSE</strong> (statement says "may change"; it actually never changes)</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>D:</strong> The second MST is obtained by finding the best swap for each non-tree edge e, adding e to T creates a cycle, and we remove the maximum weight edge in that cycle (other than e). The best such swap gives the second MST. This is a known result. <strong>TRUE</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Correct answer: A, B, D</strong></p>`
