            type: "MSQ",
            text: `<p>Consider the following statements about complexity classes:</p>
<p>Which statements is/are <strong>TRUE</strong>?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>If any NP-Complete problem can be solved in polynomial time, then P = NP</p>`,
                `<p>The problem "Given a graph G, does G have a Hamiltonian path?" is in NP because a given path can be verified in polynomial time.</p>`,
                `<p>&nbsp;The Travelling Salesman Problem (decision version) is NP-Complete, which means no polynomial-time algorithm exists for it</p>`,
                `<p>Every problem in P is also in NP</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "B", "D"],
            solution: `<p><strong>A:</strong> By definition of NP-Completeness, every NP problem reduces to any NP-Complete problem in polynomial time. So if one NP-Complete problem is solvable in polynomial time, all NP problems are, meaning P = NP. <strong>TRUE</strong></p>
<p><strong>B:</strong> Hamiltonian path is in NP because given a candidate path (certificate), we can verify it visits each vertex exactly once and uses only edges in G. This takes O(n) time. <strong>TRUE</strong></p>
<p><strong>C:</strong> NP-Complete means: (1) the problem is in NP, and (2) every NP problem reduces to it. It does <strong>NOT</strong> mean no polynomial algorithm exists, which would only follow if <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">P</mi><mo>&ne;</mo><mi>NP</mi></math>, which is unproven. We believe TSP has no poly algorithm, but this is not proven. <strong>FALSE</strong></p>
<p><strong>D:</strong> Every problem in P can be solved in polynomial time. A polynomial-time algorithm is also a polynomial-time verifier. Therefore, P<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&sube;</mo></math>NP. <strong>TRUE</strong></p>
<p><strong>Correct answer: A, B, D</strong></p>`
