            answer: "D",
            solution: `<p>f(n) = o(g(n)) means <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>lim</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mo>&infin;</mo><mo>)</mo></math> f(n)/g(n) = 0.</p>
<p>f(n) = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></math> means <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>lim</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mo>&infin;</mo><mo>)</mo></math> f(n)/g(n) = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&infin;</mo></math>.</p>
<p>S1: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></math>? lim <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> = lim 1/n = 0. TRUE</p>
<p>S2: n&middot;log n = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></math>? lim <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> = lim (log n)/n = 0 (log n grows slower than n). TRUE</p>
<p>S3: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mo>^</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>^</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>)</mo></math>? lim <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mo>^</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>^</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>&infin;</mo></math> (exponential always dominates polynomial). TRUE</p>
<p>S4: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&radic;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>?</mo></math> lim <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&radic;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>/</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> = lim <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>^</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> / <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>
<p>Let n = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mo>^</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi></math>, so as <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mo>&infin;</mo></math>, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mo>&infin;</mo></math>: = lim <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mo>^</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>&infin;</mo></math>(exponential in m vs polynomial in m). <strong>TRUE</strong></p>
<p>All four statements are TRUE.</p>`
