            answer: "B",
            solution: `<p style="text-align: justify;">The fractional knapsack problem is a relaxation of the 0/1 knapsack problem. Because any valid Explanation to the 0/1 knapsack problem is also a valid Explanation to the fractional knapsack problem, the optimal value of the fractional version must be at least as large as the 0/1 version <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mi>V_F</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>V_I</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">If the greedy choice for the fractional knapsack naturally picks only whole items to fill the capacity perfectly, then that exact selection is feasible and optimal for the 0/1 version, making <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>V_F</mi></math> equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>V_I</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>`
