            type: "MCQ",
            text: `<p style="text-align: justify;">Consider a set of n elements that we want to sort using a comparison-based sorting algorithm. Suppose we have partial information about the input array: we are guaranteed that the array is already divided into <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt></math> blocks, where each block of size <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt></math> is individually sorted internally.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>What is the tightest information-theoretic lower bound on the number of comparisons needed to fully sort this array in the worst case?</strong></p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&#937;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math></p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math></p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>`,
            ],
            answer: "C",
            solution: `<p>The total combinations of arranging elements across these pre-sorted blocks match the multinomial coefficient:</p>
<p>Total Permutations <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>!</mo><mo>/</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo></math></p>
<p>Taking the base-2 logarithm to get the information-theoretic lower bound:</p>
<p>log(Total Permutations) <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>*</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo></math></p>
<p>Using Stirling's approximation</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo></math> <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>:</mo></math> = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>*</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced open="[" close="]"><mrow><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfenced></math> = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> -<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>+ n = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>= <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo></math> =<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Thus, the tightest lower bound for comparisons is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></math> which scales identically to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Omega;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math> by a constant factor but directly represents the constraint block structure.</p>`
