            answer: ["A", "C", "D"],
            solution: `<p>(A) Query P CORRECT. Uses EXCEPT to find B values in S not yet paired with this A. NOT EXISTS means "there is no B in S that is missing from R for this A value."</p>
<p>(B) Query Q INCORRECT. Counts distinct B values for each A in R and checks if it equals |S|. Counterexample:&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>COUNT</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>DISTINCTB</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>for</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>,</mo></math> but x is not paired with B=2. Query Q returns x incorrectly.</p>
<p>(C) Query R CORRECT. Double NOT EXISTS formulation: "there is no B in S such that (A, B) is NOT in R."</p>
<p>(D) Query T CORRECT. Fixes Query Q by first restricting R to only rows where&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mo>&isin;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi></math> (the WHERE clause), then counting distinct B values. Now COUNT(DISTINCT B) = |S| correctly identifies A values paired with ALL B in S.</p>`
