            solution: `<p>Conflict analysis identifies conflicts (same data item, at least one write, different transactions):</p>
<ul>
<li>R2(B) and W1(B): both on B. R2 at time 2, W1 at time 3. T2 reads before T1 writes<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>edge <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>1</mn></math>.</li>
<li>W1(B) and R3(B): W1 at time 3,&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>at</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>time</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi>edge</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo></math></li>
<li>R1(A) and W2(A): R1 at time 1, W2 at time 5<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>edge <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>2</mn></math>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Precedence graph: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mn>3</mn></math>. Cycle between T1 and T2.<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>(A) FALSE.</p>
<p>Recoverability: A schedule is recoverable if whenever Ti reads data written by Tj, Tj commits before Ti commits.</p>
<ul>
<li>T2 reads B (R2(B) at time 2). At time 2, W1(B) has NOT yet happened (W1(B) is at time 3). So T2 reads B's original value, not a dirty read from T1. No dependency.</li>
<li>T3 reads B (R3(B) at time 4. W1(B) is at time 3. T3 reads T1's written value. T1 commits (C1) at time 6, T3 commits (C3) at time 9. C1 before C3.</li>
</ul>
<p>The schedule is RECOVERABLE.<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>(B) TRUE.</p>
<p>Cascading rollback avoidance: Requires every read to be of a value written by an already-committed transaction. T3 reads B at time 4 (after W1(B) at time 3). T1 is not committed at time 4 (C1 is at time 6). T3 reads an uncommitted value.<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>(C) FALSE.</p>
<p>(D): If T1 aborts after W1(B), T1's write to B is undone. T3 read B at time 4, which was written by T1 at time 3. T3 read a value that no longer exists (dirty read). T3 must abort.<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>(D) TRUE.</p>`
