            answer: ["A", "B", "C"],
            solution: `<p>NATURAL JOIN on&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mo>)</mo></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi><mo>)</mo></math> joins on the common attribute A and outputs one copy of A, plus B and C.</p>
<p>A: Cartesian product filtered by R.A = S.A, outputs R.A, R.B, S.C. Equivalent. TRUE.</p>
<p>B: JOIN USING(A) joins on A and outputs a single A column. Equivalent. TRUE.</p>
<p>C: INNER JOIN ON R.A = S.A outputs R.A, B, C, same as natural join result. TRUE.</p>
<p>D: Adds DISTINCT. Natural join does not automatically deduplicate unless the result naturally has no duplicates. If R and S have duplicate tuples, DISTINCT changes the result. Not always equivalent. FALSE.</p>`
