            options: [
                `<p>The receiver cannot distinguish between a new frame and a retransmission of an old frame.</p>`,
                `<p>Sender runs out of sequence numbers before ACKs arrive.</p>`,
                `<p>The protocol becomes equivalent to stop-and-wait with window size <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math>.</p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> exceeds the physical buffer capacity of any receiver.</p>`,
            ],
            answer: "A",
            solution: `<p>With n-bit sequence numbers, there are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> distinct sequence numbers. If window size = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math>, consider this scenario: sender sends frames 0 to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, all ACKs lost.</p>
<p>Sender retransmits frame 0. Receiver has already advanced its window to expect frame 0 as a NEW frame. It cannot distinguish the retransmitted old frame 0 from the new frame 0.</p>
<p>With window <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&le;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mpadded voffset="-1px"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mpadded></msup></math>, the receiver window and the sender window never overlap dangerously; old and new frames always have distinct sequence numbers from the receiver's perspective.</p>`
