            options: [
                `<p>STP eliminates broadcast storms by blocking redundant ports.</p>`,
                `<p>The root bridge is elected based on the lowest Bridge ID, where Bridge ID = priority + MAC address.</p>`,
                `<p>A port in blocking state still forwards BPDUs.</p>`,
                `<p>STP guarantees a loop-free topology but increases convergence time after a topology change.</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "B", "D"],
            solution: `<p>A: True. STP logically blocks redundant paths; broadcast frames have no loop to circulate.</p>
<p>B: True. Bridge <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>ID</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>byte</mi></math> priority + 6-byte MAC. The lowest wins the root election.</p>
<p>C: False. Blocking ports do NOT forward data frames or BPDUs; they only LISTEN to BPDUs. This is a very commonly confused fact. Blocked ports receive BPDUs but don't forward them.</p>
<p>D: True. STP convergence (30-50 seconds with classic STP) is the main weakness RSTP fixes</p>`
