            solution: `<p>A: False. The Go-Back-N receiver has a buffer of size 1; it only accepts in-order frames. Out-of-order frames are discarded. This is the key difference from Selective Repeat.</p>
<p>B: True. Selective Repeat: sender buffers unacknowledged frames (size W), receiver buffers out-of-order frames (size W).</p>
<p>C: True. GBN receiver discards all out-of-order frames. When 4 is lost, 5,6,7 arrive out of order&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math> all discarded&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math> sender retransmits 4,5,6,7.</p>
<p>D: True. GBN max window&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup><mo>&minus;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math> (SR max window&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>)</mo></math> Students frequently swap these two.</p>`
