            text: `<p>Which of the following statements about file allocation methods are TRUE?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>Contiguous allocation supports efficient sequential AND random access</p>`,
                `<p>Linked allocation is efficient for random access</p>`,
                `<p>Indexed allocation supports both sequential and random access efficiently</p>`,
                `<p>Contiguous allocation suffers from external fragmentation</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "C", "D"],
            solution: `<p>A: Contiguous allocation stores all blocks sequentially. Sequential access = read the next block directly. Random access = compute block address directly. Both O(1). TRUE.</p>
<p>B: FALSE. Linked allocation requires following pointers from block to block to reach the nth block. Random access requires O(n) pointer traversal. Inefficient.</p>
<p>C: Indexed allocation uses an index block containing all block addresses. Sequential access = read index, then each block in order. Random access = index block gives the direct address of any block. Both are efficient. TRUE.</p>
<p>D: Contiguous allocation requires a single contiguous chunk of disk space. Over time, free space becomes fragmented into small non-contiguous pieces, causing external fragmentation. TRUE.</p>`
