            answer: ["A", "C", "D"],
            solution: `<p>This is the dangling-else grammar.</p>
<p>(A) True. The grammar is ambiguous because an else can be associated with more than one unmatched if.</p>
<p>(B) False. The grammar is not LL(1) because the productions S<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>iSeS and S<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math>iS have the same FIRST&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>set</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>}</mo><mo>,</mo></math> resulting in a FIRST/FIRST conflict.</p>
<p>(C) True. In LR parsing, the dangling-else ambiguity is conventionally resolved by preferring shift over reduce, attaching each else to the nearest unmatched if.</p>
<p>(D) True. The string iiaea is derivable:&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>&rArr;</mo><mi>iS</mi><mo>&rArr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>iSeS</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&rArr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p>`
