            answer: "B",
            solution: `<p>Grammar 1: base case&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi>ab</mi></math> gives n=1, then&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi>aSb</mi></math> adds layers&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo></math> generates&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&ge;</mo><mn>1</mn></math> only (no&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi><mo>,</mo></math> no n=0).</p>
<p>Grammar 2: base case&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi>aabb</mi></math> gives n=2 directly (skipping n=1!), plus&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>&rarr;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></math> gives n=0, so Grammar 2 generates&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&cup;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&ge;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></math> completely MISSING n=1 (the string "ab" is NOT generable by Grammar 2)</p>`
