            text: `<p>Consider the following two statements:</p>
<p>S1: Every NFA can be converted to an equivalent DFA with at most <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> states, where n is the number of NFA states.</p>
<p>S2: Every NFA with n states has an equivalent DFA with EXACTLY <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> states.</p>
<p>Which of the following is correct?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>Both S1 and S2 are TRUE</p>`,
                `<p>&nbsp;S1 is TRUE, S2 is FALSE</p>`,
                `<p>S1 is FALSE, S2 is TRUE</p>`,
                `<p>Both S1 and S2 are FALSE</p>`,
            ],
            answer: "B",
            solution: `<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>S1:</strong> Subset construction produces a DFA whose states are subsets of NFA states. The number of subsets of an n-element set is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math>. So the DFA has AT MOST <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> states (many subsets may be unreachable). TRUE.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>S2:</strong> FALSE. The DFA may have far fewer than <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> states in practice. Many subsets are unreachable from the start state. Also, minimisation may reduce further. The bound <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> is an upper bound, not an exact count. There exist NFAs with n states whose minimal equivalent DFA has exactly <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> states (these are called "hard" NFAs), but this is not true for EVERY NFA.</p>`
