            options: [
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">If M is a multi-tape TM, it is strictly more powerful than a single-tape TM (it can compute more functions)</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">A non-deterministic TM (NTM) can solve problems that a deterministic TM (DTM) cannot</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">A TM with a doubly-infinite tape (tape extends in both directions) accepts exactly the same class of languages as a standard TM</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">Every NTM can be simulated by a DTM, possibly with exponential slowdown in time</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["C", "D"],
            solution: `<p><strong>A:</strong> FALSE. Multi-tape TMs are more EFFICIENT (polynomial speedup) but not more POWERFUL. Every multi-tape TM can be simulated by a single-tape TM. Same class of languages (RE languages).</p>
<p><strong>B:</strong> FALSE. NTMs are not more powerful than DTMs in terms of languages accepted. Every NTM can be simulated by a DTM (possibly with exponential slowdown). Both accept exactly the class of RE languages. (Note: in terms of complexity, NP vs P is open but in computability, DTM = NTM in power.)</p>
<p><strong>C:</strong> TRUE. A doubly-infinite tape can be simulated by a standard (one-sided infinite) tape by interleaving the two halves. Same computational power, same class of languages.</p>
<p><strong>D:</strong> TRUE. Simulate NTM with a 3-tape DTM: tape 1= input, tape 2 = simulation of one computation path, tape 3 = BFS counter to enumerate computation trees. DTM accepts iff NTM has any accepting path. Simulation exists, though exponentially slower.</p>`
