            text: `<p>Consider the following problem:</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">{</mi><mo>⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi></math> is a TM that halts on the empty string &epsilon;}</p>
<p>Which of the following is TRUE about <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math>?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math>is decidable because we only need to simulate M on &epsilon; and check if it halts</p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math> is undecidable and is RE (recursively enumerable)</p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math> is undecidable and is NOT RE</p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math> is decidable using a universal TM</p>`,
            ],
            answer: "B",
            solution: `<p style="text-align: justify;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math> is RE: To semi-decide, simulate M on <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></math>. If M halts (accept or reject), output YES. If M loops forever, we loop forever. This is a valid semi-decider so <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math> is RE.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math> is NOT decidable: Suppose it were decidable. Then we could solve the general Halting Problem: given ⟨M, w⟩, construct a new TM M' that ignores its input, writes w on the tape, and simulates M on w. Then <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mo>⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi><mo>'</mo><mo>⟩</mo><mo>)</mo></math> = YES iff M halts on w. This reduces HALT to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></math> but HALT is undecidable <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&rarr;</mo><msub><mi>HALT</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></msub></math> is undecidable.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">A: WRONG simulating M on &epsilon; does NOT give a decider. If M loops on <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&epsilon;</mi></math>, our simulation also loops. We cannot decide halting by simulation alone.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">D: Wrong, a universal TM just simulates; it doesn't decide halting.</p>`
