            text: `<p>Let&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A_TM</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mo>⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">w</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>accepts</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">w</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>}</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>TOTAL_TM</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>{</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>:</mo></math> M halts on all inputs }. A student claims that since both A and B are "about halting behaviour of Turing machines," they must sit at the same level of undecidability and hence be mutually many-one reducible in both directions. Examine this claim carefully against the arithmetical hierarchy and select all statements below that are actually TRUE.</p>`,
