            answer: "B",
            solution: `<p>The language consists of strings of the form&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msup></math> satisfying&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>An NPDA can process the string deterministically in phases: while reading a's, it pushes one stack symbol for each a. While reading b's, it pops one stack symbol for each b. After the b-block, while reading c's, it pops one stack symbol for each c. The condition&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></math> is therefore exactly equivalent to the requirement that all pushed symbols are consumed by the end of the input.</p>
<p>Hence L is context-free.</p>
<p>L is not regular because it requires an unbounded numerical comparison. For example, intersect L with the regular language&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mo>*</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi><mo>*</mo></math> (equivalently, restrict k = 0 through intersection with a<em>b</em>) to obtain&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&ge;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></math> which is not regular. Therefore L itself cannot be regular.</p>
<p>The cases involving zero values of i, j, or k are already handled by the same construction, so the restriction&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>&ge;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>1</mn></math> is unnecessary.</p>`
