            solution: `<p style="text-align: justify;">Total data = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>3</mn><mo>&times;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>20</mn></msup></math> bytes. Each DMA transfer moves 8 bytes, so the number of DMA transfers is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&times;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>20</mn></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>393216</mn></math>. Each transfer occupies 2 bus cycles, so total DMA-occupied bus cycles = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>393216</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>&times;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>786432</mn></math> cycles. Since the disk rate is 12 MiB/s, the total transfer time is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>MiB</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>MiB</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi><mo>)</mo></math> <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></math>. During 0.25 s, the CPU executes <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>400</mn><mo>&times;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&times;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn></math>= 100000000 CPU cycles. Therefore, the fraction of CPU cycles lost = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>786432</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>100000000</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>&times;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>786432</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Rounded to four decimal places, the answer is 0.7864%.</p>`
