            text: `<p>Regarding static and dynamic hazards in combinational circuits, which of the following statements are correct?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>A static-1 hazard occurs when the output is expected to remain constant at logic 1 during a single-variable input transition, but momentarily glitches to 0 due to unequal path delays.</p>`,
                `<p>Static and dynamic hazards can occur only in sequential circuits and are impossible in purely combinational logic.</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">A static-1 hazard in a two-level SOP realization can be eliminated by adding a redundant consensus product term that bridges the two adjacent product-term groupings involved in the transition.</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">A dynamic hazard occurs when the output is required to change value once but instead switches multiple times <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&rarr;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math> during a single input transition, indicating multiple unequal-length signal paths.</p>`,
            ],
