            type: "MSQ",
            text: `<p>With respect to prime implicants and Boolean function minimization theory, which of the following statements are correct?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">A prime implicant is a product term that cannot be combined with any other adjacent term to eliminate an additional literal while still implying the function.</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">An essential prime implicant is one that covers at least one minterm of the function that is not covered by any other prime implicant.</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">The total number of prime implicants of a Boolean function is always exactly equal to the number of product terms appearing in its minimal SOP expression.</p>`,
                `<p style="text-align: justify;">Don't-care conditions may be used while forming and combining prime implicants (to allow larger groupings), but need not be covered by the final selected minimal set of implicants.</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "B", "D"],
            solution: `<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>(A) True:</strong> definition of a prime implicant.<br /><strong>(B) True:</strong>&nbsp;definition of an essential prime implicant.<br /><strong>(C) False:</strong>a function can have several non-essential prime implicants beyond those actually used in the minimal SOP; the minimal expression uses only the essential PIs plus the minimum subset of non-essential PIs needed to cover remaining minterms, which is often fewer than the total number of prime implicants that exist.<br /><strong>(D) True:</strong>&nbsp;don't-cares help form larger/simpler groupings but are never mandatory to cover in the final chosen implicant set.</p>`
