            text: `<p>Regarding the theoretical distinction between Mealy and Moore finite state machines, which of the following statements are correct?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>In a Moore machine, the output depends only on the present state, whereas in a Mealy machine, the output depends on both the present state and the current input.</p>`,
                `<p>An equivalent Moore machine realising the same input-output behaviour as a given Mealy machine generally requires an equal or greater number of states than the original Mealy machine, never fewer.</p>`,
                `<p>For any given Mealy machine, the output-equivalent Moore machine is guaranteed to have the same number of states as the Mealy machine, with no possibility of requiring additional states.</p>`,
                `<p>In a Mealy machine, the output can change asynchronously in direct response to an input change even without a clock edge occurring (since output depends on input as well as state), whereas a Moore machine's output remains stable between clock edges since it depends only on the present state.</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "B", "D"],
            solution: `<p><strong>(A) True:</strong> defining distinction between the two machine types.</p>
<p><strong>(B) True:</strong> converting Mealy to Moore may require splitting states based on differing incoming output values, generally requiring equal-or-more states.</p>
<p><strong>(C) False:</strong> this contradicts (B); a Moore-equivalent machine can require strictly MORE states than the original Mealy machine.</p>
<p><strong>(D) True:</strong> well-known practical distinction affecting output glitch behaviour.</p>`
