            type: "MSQ",
            text: `<p>Q. Regarding excitation tables and inter-flip-flop conversion, which of the following statements are correct?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>To realise a T flip-flop's behaviour using a D flip-flop, the required excitation is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>XOR</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Q</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>`,
                `<p>To realise a D flip-flop's behaviour using a JK flip-flop, the required excitation is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mo>'</mo><mo>.</mo></math></p>`,
                `<p>To realise an SR flip-flop's behaviour using a JK flip-flop, simply connecting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi></math> correctly reproduces true SR flip-flop behaviour for ALL input combinations, including <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p>`,
                `<p>To realise a JK flip-flop's behaviour using a T flip-flop plus external combinational logic, the required toggle excitation is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>JQ</mi><mo>'</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>KQ</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "B", "D"],
            solution: `<p><strong>(A) True :</strong> D-from-T conversion.</p>
<p><strong>(B) True:</strong> D-from-JK conversion.</p>
<p><strong>(C) False: </strong>a true SR flip-flop is undefined (forbidden) at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, but a JK flip-flop with <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math> deterministically TOGGLES at that input, which is different behaviour from the "forbidden/undefined" SR case. So <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi></math> only correctly emulates SR for the 3 valid input combinations,</p>
<p>NOT for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>&mdash; the claim of validity "for all combinations including <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>"</mo></math> is false.</p>
<p><strong>(D) True:</strong> T-from-JK conversion (derived by substituting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">Q</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Q</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>XOR</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi></math> into the JK characteristic equation).</p>`
