            neg: 0.66,
            type: "MCQ",
            text: `<p style="text-align: justify;">A non-pipelined processor has stage delays (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB) = 200, 150, 250, 200, 100 ps, so one instruction takes the sum of these delays. A 5-stage pipelined version has each stage timed by the slowest stage plus 20 ps of pipeline-register overhead. Assumptions:</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">30% of instructions are loads. Of these, 50% are immediately followed by a dependent instruction, causing a 1-cycle stall.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">20% of instructions are branches. Branches are predicted not-taken, and 60% of branches are actually taken. A taken branch costs 2 stall cycles, and a not-taken branch costs 0.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">There are no other hazards.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">What is the speedup of the pipelined processor over the non-pipelined processor (rounded to 2 decimals)?</p>`,
            image: "",
