            type: "NAT",
            text: `<p>An undirected weighted graph on vertices 1, &hellip;, 6 has edges (1,2)=3, (1,3)=5, (2,3)=1, (2,4)=7, (3,4)=4, (3,5)=6, (4,5)=2, (4,6)=8, (5,6)=3. The minimum spanning tree is unique. What is the weight of the lightest spanning tree different from it?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
            ],
            answer: 15,
            solution: `<ul>
<li>MST edges: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></math> <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo></math> <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>with weight 13.</li>
<li>The best second tree swaps one non-tree edge in for the heaviest edge on its tree cycle. The cost increase is the non-tree edge&rsquo;s weight minus that heaviest edge:
<ul>
<li>(1,3)=5 against max 3: +2</li>
<li>(3,5)=6 against max 4: +2</li>
<li>(2,4)=7 against max 4: +3</li>
<li>(4,6)=8 against max 3: +5</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>The minimum increase is 2, so the answer is 13 + 2 = <strong>15</strong>.</li>
</ul>`
