            answer: ["B", "D"],
            solution: `<p><strong>(A) False: </strong>At recursion level <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi></math>, the total non-recursive work is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></math>[cite: 2]. Summing over <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math> levels gives</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.[cite: 2]</p>
<p><strong>(B) True:</strong> Let <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msup></math>[cite: 2]. At level <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi></math>, the total work is</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi></msup><mo>&times;</mo><mo>[</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi></msup><mo>)</mo><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></math>[cite: 2]</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>/</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></math>.[cite: 2]</p>
<p>Summing over the internal levels gives</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&times;</mo><msubsup><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math>,[cite: 2]</p>
<p>because the series <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&sum;</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></math> is bounded by a constant[cite: 2].</p>
<p>The leaves contribute <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math> as well[cite: 2]. Hence <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.[cite: 2]</p>
<p><strong>(C) False:</strong> <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> gives</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.[cite: 2]</p>
<p><strong>(D) True:</strong> For <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, the exponent of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math> is repeatedly halved[cite: 2]. This takes <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">&Theta;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">log</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></math> steps[cite: 2].</p>
<p>Correct Answer: (B), (D)</p>`
