            type: "MSQ",
            text: `<p>Consider the poset <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mn>360</mn></msub></math> of positive divisors of 360 under divisibility, which is a lattice with meet = gcd and join = lcm. An element <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>&ne;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>1</mn></math> is join-irreducible if a = b &or; c implies a = b or a = c. Which of the following is/are correct?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>The number of maximal chains from 1 to 360 is 60.</p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mn>360</mn></msub></math> is a complemented lattice.</p>`,
                `<p>Exactly 12 elements of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mn>360</mn></msub><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>have a complement.</p>`,
                `<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mn>360</mn></msub><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>has exactly 6 join-irreducible elements.</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["A", "D"],
            solution: `<ul>
<li><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>360</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&middot;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&middot;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo></math></li>
<li>(A) A maximal chain adds one prime factor at a time, giving <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfrac><mrow><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>&middot;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>&middot;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo></mrow></mfrac></math>= 60 arrangements, so (A) is true.</li>
<li>(B) False: 2 has no complement, because we would need y with gcd(2,y) = 1 and lcm(2,y) = 360.</li>
<li>(C) False: x has a complement iff gcd<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>360</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math> = 1 (a unitary divisor), and there are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>8</mn></math> of these, not 12.</li>
<li>(D) True: the join-irreducibles are the prime powers 2, 4, 8, 3, 9, 5, which is 6 elements.</li>
</ul>`
