            type: "MSQ",
            text: `<p>&nbsp;Let G be a connected weighted graph. Which of the following is/are correct?</p>`,
            image: "",
            options: [
                `<p>If G has a unique minimum spanning tree, then all edge weights of G must be distinct.</p>`,
                `<p>Adding the same constant to every edge weight does not change the set of minimum spanning trees of G.</p>`,
                `<p>Adding the same positive constant to every edge weight does not change the set of shortest paths between any pair of vertices.</p>`,
                `<p>If all weights are positive, replacing each weight w by w&sup2; preserves the set of MSTs, but may change some shortest paths.</p>`,
            ],
            answer: ["B", "D"],
            solution: `<p><strong>(A) False: </strong>A graph can have a unique MST even when some edge weights are equal. Equal weights do not necessarily create alternative minimum spanning trees.</p>
<p><strong>(B) True: </strong>Every spanning tree on n vertices contains exactly n &minus; 1 edges. Adding a constant c to every edge weight increases the weight of every spanning tree by exactly <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>&minus;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Therefore, the set of MSTs remains unchanged.</p>
<p><strong>(C) False: </strong>Consider a direct edge of weight 7 and an alternative two-edge path with weights 3 and 3. Initially, the two-edge path costs 6 and is shorter than the direct edge of weight 7.</p>
<p>After adding 3 to every edge weight, the direct edge costs 10, while the two-edge path costs 12. The shortest path changes.</p>
<p><strong>(D) True: </strong>Squaring positive edge weights preserves their relative order because w&sup2; is strictly increasing for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">w</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Therefore, the set of MSTs remains unchanged.</p>
<p>However, shortest paths may change because their costs depend on sums of edge weights. For example, a direct edge of weight 5 is initially shorter than a two-edge path with weights 3 and 3, whose total is 6. After squaring, the direct edge costs 25,</p>
<p>while the two-edge path costs 9 + 9 = 18. The preferred path therefore changes.</p>`
